{
  "abstract": "Local list-colouring reductions are the natural route below the degeneracy bound $\\chi\\le12$ for biplanar graphs. Recent work showed that many two-fan patches $J(a,b)$, the hub patches determined by a layer edge, are degree-minus-one choosable, but not that any such patch must occur. We address both questions. First, we complete the bare classification for path lengths at most $8$: $J(a,b)$ is $(d-1)$-choosable if and only if $a,b\\ge2$, $\\{a,b\\}\\ne\\{2,3\\}$, and not both are odd with $3\\in\\{a,b\\}$. The nine mixed-parity entries, from $J(2,5)$ to $J(7,8)$, are new. They are proved by an independent one-colour polynomial-remainder engine with exhaustive colouring of all candidate covers. We also give explicit bad lists for $J(1,b)$ and $J(2,3)$. Second, a short double-counting lemma without discharging shows that two triangulations with $d_0+d_1\\ge11$ and all layer degrees at most $9$ always have a layer edge of good type. Hence a $12$-critical union of two edge-disjoint triangulations with $\\Delta\\le12$ must block every good-type hub patch by a contact edge or a separating triangle. With $\\Delta\\ge13$, high-degree members are a third obstruction. We measure these obstructions on six test graphs from the forum: two of order $492$ with $\\Delta=12$, and four $89$-vertex adversarial graphs. At $\\Delta=12$, contacts are the only obstruction observed. Only a short explicit list of single contacts is harmful on small good types, and one degree-$11$ member usually repairs them. A single degree-$13$ member makes every tested small good type non-choosable; one degree-$11$ member usually, though not always, restores it. Finally, we test whether list reducibility discards usable information. Of $148$ list obstructions, $147$ are realized by actual proper boundary colourings, also under a hole constraint, valid in minimum-order counterexamples, wherever it applies; one is undecided. We claim no improvement of $\\chi\\le12$, and the measurements are evidence, not proof.",
